  <section id="guide" class="relative w-full">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
      <header class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold mb-2 text-lightblue">A Practical Guide to Snorkeling in Bali</h2>
        <p class="text-white/80">Ready to explore beneath the waves? Bali isn't just a world-class diving destination; it's a snorkeler's paradise. This guide cuts through the noise to give you practical, field-tested advice on the best sites, essential safety protocols, and how to make the most of your time in the water.</p>
      </header>

      <article class="prose prose-invert max-w-none">
        <p>Bali's volcanic origins and its prime location within the Coral Triangle have created an underwater ecosystem teeming with life. Whether you're a complete beginner looking for calm, fish-filled bays or an experienced water enthusiast seeking a thrilling drift over vibrant reefs, the island has a spot for you. Let's dive in.</p>

        <br>
        <h3 class="text-lightblue">Why Choose Bali for Your Snorkeling Adventure?</h3>
        <p>Bali offers a unique combination of factors that make it a top-tier snorkeling destination:</p>
        <ul class="marker:text-gold !pl-5 !space-y-3">
          <li><i class="fa-solid fa-seedling text-gold mr-3 w-5 text-center"></i><strong>Incredible Biodiversity:</strong> Expect to see everything from tiny clownfish hiding in anemones to majestic sea turtles and, if you're lucky, even giant manta rays.</li>
          <li><i class="fa-solid fa-umbrella-beach text-gold mr-3 w-5 text-center"></i><strong>Diverse Sites for All Levels:</strong> Enjoy easy shore entries from sandy beaches or take a traditional *jukung* boat to explore hidden coves and dramatic coral walls.</li>
          <li><i class="fa-solid fa-ship text-gold mr-3 w-5 text-center"></i><strong>Unique Underwater Landmarks:</strong> Where else in the world can you snorkel over a 125-meter-long US Army shipwreck just a few meters from the shore?</li>
          <li><i class="fa-solid fa-wallet text-gold mr-3 w-5 text-center"></i><strong>Accessible & Great Value:</strong> Organising a snorkeling trip is straightforward and can be tailored to any budget, from simple gear rental to all-inclusive guided tours.</li>
        </ul>
        
        <br>
        <h3 class="text-lightblue">Bali's Top Snorkeling Havens: A Site-by-Site Breakdown</h3>
        <p>Not all snorkeling spots are created equal. Matching the site to your skill level and interest is key to an amazing experience. Here are our top picks:</p>
        
        <h4>Blue Lagoon, Padang Bai — A Beginner's Paradise</h4>
        <p>Tucked away on the east coast, Blue Lagoon is the quintessential spot for first-timers and families. Its sheltered, calm waters and sandy bottom make for an easy and relaxing experience. The coral starts just a few feet from the shore, sloping gently and attracting a fantastic array of reef fish like angelfish, butterflyfish, and Moorish idols. Visibility is often excellent, making it feel like you're swimming in an aquarium.</p>
        <ul class="!list-none !pl-0">
          <li><i class="fa-solid fa-user-check text-gold w-5 mr-2"></i><strong>Best for:</strong> Beginners, families, underwater photography.</li>
          <li><i class="fa-solid fa-fish-fins text-gold w-5 mr-2"></i><strong>What to see:</strong> Cuttlefish, moray eels, pufferfish, and vibrant soft corals.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Amed & the USAT Liberty Wreck, Tulamben — A Brush with History</h4>
        <p>For a truly unique snorkel, head to Tulamben. The USAT Liberty shipwreck, torpedoed in WWII, now rests just off the rocky shore. The highest point of the wreck is only about 3 meters (10 feet) below the surface, making this world-famous dive site incredibly accessible to snorkelers. The black volcanic sand provides a dramatic contrast to the corals and sponges that have completely encrusted the ship, which is now home to schools of jackfish, bumphead parrotfish, and countless smaller critters.</p>
        <ul class="!list-none !pl-0">
          <li><i class="fa-solid fa-anchor text-gold w-5 mr-2"></i><strong>Best for:</strong> Confident swimmers, history buffs, unique photo opportunities.</li>
          <li><i class="fa-solid fa-camera-retro text-gold w-5 mr-2"></i><strong>What to see:</strong> The shipwreck structure, large schools of fish, garden eels.</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h4>Nusa Penida & Lembongan — The Big Fish Encounter</h4>
        <p>A short boat ride from the mainland, the Nusa islands offer a more adventurous experience. Spots like Manta Bay and Crystal Bay are famous for their encounters with majestic manta rays, which come to feed and be cleaned. The currents here can be strong and conditions changeable, so this area is best for experienced snorkelers and always with a professional guide. The reward is the chance to swim alongside some of the ocean's most graceful giants.</p>
        <ul class="!list-none !pl-0">
          <li><i class="fa-solid fa-water text-gold w-5 mr-2"></i><strong>Best for:</strong> Advanced snorkelers, adventure seekers, manta ray lovers.</li>
          <li><i class="fa-solid fa-person-swimming text-gold w-5 mr-2"></i><strong>What to see:</strong> Manta rays, seasonal Mola Mola (oceanic sunfish), healthy coral reefs.</li>
        </ul>
        
        <br>
        <h3 class="text-lightblue">Planning Your Underwater Expedition</h3>
        <p>A little preparation goes a long way. Here's what you need to know before you get your fins wet.</p>

        <h4>The Best Season for Snorkeling</h4>
        <p>The prime time for snorkeling in Bali is during the <strong>dry season, from April to October</strong>. During these months, you'll find the calmest seas, the least rain, and consistently excellent underwater visibility. The shoulder months (April, May, October) are often perfect, with great conditions and fewer crowds.</p>

        <h4>Ocean Etiquette & Safety First</h4>
        <p>The golden rule of snorkeling is to be a passive observer. This protects both you and the fragile marine environment.</p>
        <ul class="marker:text-gold !pl-5 !space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Look, Don't Touch:</strong> Coral is a living animal and can be killed by a simple touch. Admire marine life from a respectful distance and never chase or harass animals.</li>
          <li><strong>Mind Your Fins:</strong> Be aware of your surroundings. A careless kick can stir up sand, reducing visibility, or break decades of coral growth in seconds.</li>
          <li><strong>Use Reef-Safe Sunscreen:</strong> Standard sunscreens contain chemicals that are toxic to coral reefs. Choose a mineral-based (zinc oxide or titanium dioxide) sunscreen to protect your skin and the ocean.</li>
          <li><strong>Listen to Your Guide:</strong> Always attend the pre-snorkel briefing. Local guides have invaluable knowledge about currents, conditions, and potential hazards.</li>
        </ul>

        <br>
        <h3 class="text-lightblue" id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h3>
        <div class="space-y-3">
          
      <div class="border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 bg-white/5">
        <button class="w-full flex items-center justify-between text-left" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden')">
          <span class="font-semibold">Is snorkeling in Bali suitable for beginners?</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-sm"></i>
        </button>
        <div class="hidden mt-3 text-white/80">Absolutely! Bali is one of the best places to learn. Start at sheltered spots like Blue Lagoon in Padang Bai, where the waters are calm and clear. We always recommend going with a reputable guide who can provide a safety briefing and point out marine life.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 bg-white/5">
        <button class="w-full flex items-center justify-between text-left" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden')">
          <span class="font-semibold">What’s the ideal season for snorkeling in Bali?</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-sm"></i>
        </button>
        <div class="hidden mt-3 text-white/80">The prime snorkeling season is during Bali's dry season, from April to October. You'll find the calmest seas and best visibility. However, Bali offers year-round opportunities; just be mindful that the rainy season (November-March) can bring choppier conditions.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 bg-white/5">
        <button class="w-full flex items-center justify-between text-left" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden')">
          <span class="font-semibold">How much does snorkeling in Bali typically cost?</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-sm"></i>
        </button>
        <div class="hidden mt-3 text-white/80">Costs range from a simple gear rental on the beach for a few dollars to a full-day private boat trip. A typical guided shore-snorkeling trip might cost around $30-50 USD, while a boat trip to places like Nusa Penida could be $60-100+ USD per person, often including lunch and equipment. Always confirm what's included.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 bg-white/5">
        <button class="w-full flex items-center justify-between text-left" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden')">
          <span class="font-semibold">What essential gear should I bring for snorkeling?</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-sm"></i>
        </button>
        <div class="hidden mt-3 text-white/80">Your basic mask, snorkel, and fins are key. We highly recommend a long-sleeved rash guard for sun protection and, crucially, <strong>reef-safe sunscreen</strong> to protect Bali's delicate coral. An underwater camera is also a great addition to capture the memories.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 bg-white/5">
        <button class="w-full flex items-center justify-between text-left" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden')">
          <span class="font-semibold">Can I try scuba diving if I enjoy snorkeling?</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-sm"></i>
        </button>
        <div class="hidden mt-3 text-white/80">Yes, snorkeling is the perfect gateway to scuba diving. Many guests try snorkeling, fall in love with the underwater world, and then sign up for a Discover Scuba Diving experience or an Open Water Diver course. It's a great way to build confidence in the water.</div>
      </div>        </div>
